$4 \mathrm{ L} \alpha \Delta \mathrm{T}=3 \mathrm{ L}\left(\alpha_{\mathrm{C}}\right) \Delta \mathrm{T}
\alpha_C=\frac{4 \alpha}{3}$

The radiated power of a body, $P_1=1000 \mathrm{ W}$
Temperature, $T_1=400 \mathrm{ K}$
As, we know that according to Stefan's Boltzmann's law,
Radiated power $\propto$ (Temperature $)^4\therefore$ If the radiated power at temperature $T_2=800 \mathrm{ K}$ is $P_2$, then $\frac{P_2}{P_1}=\left(\frac{T_2}{T_1}\right)^4 \begin{aligned}
\Rightarrow \quad$ P_2 & =P_1 $\times\left(\frac{T_2}{T_1}\right)^4=1000 \times\left(\frac{800}{400}\right)^4 \\ \text{$ and $} =16000 \mathrm{ W}
\end{aligned}$

